Z||000005||Phaeton||06-01-2000||03:05 AM||||Work back from the front running lights' harness since that has a problem for sure.  This sounds like a direct short to ground so hopefully it won't be inside the harnes but at one end or the other.  If it's in the middle of the harness there may be an obvious worn spot in the conduit.<P>Then again, the reversed action of the jamb switch should be corrected first, and may even end the search.<P>Sure do wish electricity could be seen.||||reg||12
Z||000006||Phaeton||06-01-2000||03:07 AM||||OH, hazards blink all together.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000007||redfiero||06-01-2000||07:02 AM||||dr,<P>Just worked through a similar problem with Hoop.  The socket in the rear of the front lights will have three wires going into them.  There will be a blue, brown and black.  The blue and brown go together and enter into the rear of the socket.  The black wire enters the side of the socket.  The problem is that the black wire is not grounded.  The connector will be broken.<P>A test:  Pull the front sockets.  Turn the blinkers on(which ever direction doesn't work).  Pull the black wire from the socket.  Ground the socket (where the black wire was) to the frame.  Your blinker should work.<P>Good Luck,<BR>matt||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||06-01-2000||02:00 PM||||For the interior light and radio problem check the link below. Under articles and troubleshooting there is a page on "gremlins". There is an explanation of how a blown fuse can cause wierd things to happen between the radio and inside lights.<P>Good luck.<P><BR> <A HREF="http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.franken.de/users/dagobah/luke/fieroidx.html</A> ||||reg||1
